// DOC-82, TM-05 sec. 2.10 [DECK-SOURCED]. Mirvis slide 36, continuing SOURCE 5 pt. II.C.
// PROVENANCE: every quotation on this slide is from SOURCE 5, the December 2, 2024 opinion
// denying the Motion to Revise, as the court's slip opinion -- not verified against the
// published reporter text at 326 A.3d 1203.
#slide[
  === Ground three continued: MFW and ab initio.

  #set text(18pt)

  - "Defendants did not follow MFW here." #pause

  - The "central objective of the MFW standard is to provide an incentive for controllers to embrace the procedural approach most favorable to minority investors." Those protections "are both an independent, adequately empowered special committee that fulfills its duty of care and an uncoerced, informed vote of a majority of the minority~stockholders." #pause

  - "To achieve that objective, the controller must precommit to MFW protections. In the parlance of case law, the transaction must be conditioned 'ab initio' or 'before the start of negotiations' on the dual~protections." #pause

  - Having them "at the start of economic negotiations" is "essential" to replicate a third-party process, because it prevents a controller from using MFW as a bargaining~chip.
]

// DOC-83, TM-05 sec. 2.10 [DECK-SOURCED]. Mirvis slide 37; SOURCE 5 pt. II.D is headed "The
// Proxy Statement Is Materially Misleading," which his slide transcribes correctly.
// "BOOTSTRAP????" is his own annotation on the slide, not part of the quoted material.
// PROVENANCE: every quotation on this slide is from SOURCE 5, the December 2, 2024 opinion
// denying the Motion to Revise, as the court's slip opinion -- not verified against the
// published reporter text at 326 A.3d 1203.
#slide[
  === Ground four: the proxy statement, and the bootstrap.

  #set text(18pt)

  - The heading: "The Proxy Statement Is Materially Misleading." To be fully informed for ratification purposes, "the stockholders must be told specifically . . . what the binding effect of a favorable vote will~be." #pause

  - Tesla told them their vote could "extinguish claims for breach of fiduciary duty by authorizing an act that otherwise would constitute a breach," and that "a new stockholder vote allows the disclosure deficiencies found by the Tornetta court to be corrected, among other~things." #pause

  - The court: "All of this is materially false or misleading. . . . [R]atification cannot be deployed post-trial to extinguish an adjudicated breach of the duty of loyalty. It cannot cleanse a conflicted-controller transaction absent the full suite of MFW protections. At best . . . ratification shifts the burden of proof." #pause

  #callout[The 2024 proxy asked stockholders to cure a defect by telling them that their vote would cure~it.]
]

